# Chapter 1

# Before the Child Is Born

There is a moment when a child becomes visible.

It may be the first ultrasound. A small flicker on a screen. A heartbeat that is suddenly no longer an abstraction. Or it may be much later: the first movement felt beneath a hand, the first photograph, the first cry in a delivery room.

We are drawn to such moments because they give beginnings a shape.

But biology is rarely so accommodating.

By the time parents hear a heartbeat, cells have already divided countless times. Organs are beginning to take form. The nervous system is already following an extraordinarily complicated developmental program. The placenta is regulating what passes between maternal and fetal circulation. The parents themselves may already be changing—physically, psychologically, socially, and neurologically.

The child has not yet been born.

Development has already begun.

This is where our story starts.

Not because everything important happens before birth.

Not because the future can be read from a fetus.

And certainly not because parents can control the conditions of development with sufficient discipline.

We begin here because the developmental timeline begins here.

That distinction will matter throughout this book.

One of the great temptations of developmental science is to take a true observation and turn it into a much larger claim than the evidence can bear.

A prenatal exposure may influence birth weight. That does not mean it determines intelligence.

A parental characteristic may be associated with a psychiatric diagnosis. That does not mean it caused the disorder.

A biological difference may be measurable years later. That does not mean it represents permanent damage.

An intervention may improve an outcome in a clinical trial. That does not mean every family needs it, that it will work everywhere, or that its effects will persist.

And when science identifies something that genuinely matters, the final question is not simply:

**What can parents do?**

It is also:

**What can parents not do—and what must society do instead?**

That is the question that will guide this book.

---

## A child does not begin at birth

The conventional story of parenting begins when the child arrives.

The developmental story does not.

Pregnancy occupies a strange position in our imagination. It is simultaneously intensely biological and intensely social. Something is developing inside a body, but that development is taking place within relationships, institutions, economies, homes, workplaces, healthcare systems, and cultures.

The World Health Organization's nurturing-care framework describes early development through a set of conditions that include health, nutrition, security and safety, responsive caregiving, and opportunities for learning. These conditions do not suddenly appear when a baby is born. They are embedded in the circumstances surrounding pregnancy and early parenthood.

That gives us our first principle:

> **Development may begin biologically before birth without being determined before birth.**

The difference is enormous.

Development is a process.

A newborn is not an unfinished adult waiting for the remaining pieces to be installed. Nor is the fetus an already-completed person whose future characteristics can be predicted from a handful of prenatal measurements.

The developing organism is continuously responding to its environment.

And so are the parents.

---

# The first environment

Before the child has a home, there is already an environment.

Before there are family routines, there is maternal physiology.

Before there is language, there are signals.

Before there is conscious memory, there is biological development.

One of the most useful examples is the placenta.

The placenta is not simply a passive filter between mother and fetus. It is an active biological interface. Among its many functions is the regulation of glucocorticoids, including cortisol. The enzyme 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 helps convert active maternal glucocorticoids into less active forms, contributing to a protective barrier between maternal stress physiology and the developing fetus.

This is a beautiful example of the way biology complicates simple stories.

A pregnant person's experience does not simply flow directly into a baby's brain.

There are barriers, buffers, adaptations, feedback systems, and thresholds.

The fetus is not exposed to the world in the same way that an adult is exposed to the world.

The developing system is mediated.

That does not mean the prenatal environment is irrelevant. Under conditions of severe or chronic stress, physiological changes can alter placental function, and researchers have identified biological pathways through which maternal distress may influence fetal growth and development.

But the existence of a mechanism is not the same thing as a prediction.

A mechanism tells us that something *can* happen.

It does not tell us that it *will* happen to a particular child.

And it certainly does not tell us that a parent is morally responsible for controlling every fluctuation in their physiology.

This distinction will become one of the book's recurring themes:

> **Biological relevance is not the same thing as parental control.**

---

# Before conception

The developmental story reaches back even further.

Some of the biological material with which a child begins life is assembled before conception. The genetic contribution of both parents is relevant. So, in different ways, are reproductive age, health, behavior, and the conditions in which conception occurs.

This is particularly clear in research on paternal age.

Sperm production involves continual cell division. As paternal age increases, the number of opportunities for replication errors also increases. Whole-genome sequencing of parent-offspring trios demonstrated a striking relationship between paternal age and the number of de novo mutations appearing in offspring. The estimated increase is on the order of two additional mutations per year of paternal age.

The underlying biology is real.

That does not mean the popular version of the story is correct.

Advanced paternal age has been associated with increased risks for several psychiatric and neurodevelopmental outcomes, including schizophrenia and autism-spectrum conditions. But those epidemiological associations cannot simply be interpreted as the consequences of sperm aging. Family characteristics, socioeconomic conditions, reproductive selection, assortative mating, and inherited genetic liability can all contribute to the association.

This is where developmental science becomes uncomfortable—and more interesting.

A finding can be biologically true while a simplified causal explanation is wrong.

The number of new mutations increases with paternal age.

That does not mean paternal age determines a child's future.

The difference between those statements is exactly the difference between science and mythology.

### The lesson of paternal age

The appropriate conclusion is not:

*Have children early or risk damaging them.*

It is:

*Reproductive biology changes with age; some risks change with it; those risks are generally probabilistic rather than deterministic; and their interpretation requires attention to absolute risk, inherited liability, and the wider family context.*

That is a much less dramatic message.

It is also much closer to the evidence.

---

# The body before the baby

Preconception health provides another opportunity to distinguish what science supports from what popular culture demands.

Some interventions have exceptionally clear evidence.

Folic acid is one of them.

Taking folic acid before conception and during early pregnancy substantially reduces the risk of neural tube defects. This is among the clearest examples in reproductive medicine of a defined intervention producing a defined preventive effect.

The lesson is powerful precisely because it is limited.

Folic acid prevents neural tube defects.

It does not prove that a supplement can optimize a child's intelligence.

It does not establish a formula for producing emotional resilience.

It does not turn pregnancy into an engineering problem.

Good science often works this way.

It gives us something narrower than the story we wanted, but more reliable than the story we imagined.

Preconception care can also involve identifying and managing conditions such as diabetes, hypertension, thyroid disorders, psychiatric illness, medication exposure, substance use, and nutritional risks. The goal is not to manufacture a perfect pregnancy. It is to reduce identifiable and modifiable risks.

That difference in language matters.

**Risk reduction** is not **risk elimination**.

**Preparation** is not **control**.

And **prevention** is not **optimization of the human being**.

---

# The problem with anxious science

Few areas of modern parenting produce more anxiety than prenatal stress.

The basic story sounds convincing.

Stress changes cortisol.

Cortisol can cross biological boundaries.

The fetal brain is developing rapidly.

Therefore, stress during pregnancy must damage the baby's future.

The problem is that every step after the first two becomes progressively less certain.

Observational studies have often found associations between prenatal anxiety or stress and later emotional and behavioral outcomes. But when researchers use genetically informed designs, sibling comparisons, adoption approaches, IVF designs, and other forms of causal triangulation, many apparently straightforward associations become smaller or disappear.

A genetically informed meta-analysis reported essentially no prenatal anxiety association with offspring internalizing after accounting for genetic relatedness, while postnatal parent-child associations remained small but detectable.

This does not prove that every form of prenatal distress is harmless.

It tells us something more useful:

**we cannot treat all prenatal psychological associations as evidence of direct fetal programming.**

There are at least three possibilities.

The exposure may have a direct biological effect during pregnancy.

The apparent association may reflect inherited characteristics shared by parent and child.

Or the prenatal period may be connected to the postnatal environment in ways that continue after birth.

In reality, all three pathways may coexist.

This is why causal research matters.

A correlation gives us a clue.

A well-designed comparison tells us more.

Triangulation gives us confidence.

And sometimes the scientifically important result is that an attractive explanation becomes weaker under better methods.

That is not a failure of science.

It is science doing its job.

---

# The pregnant person is not a laboratory instrument

There is another reason to be careful here.

A developmental explanation can become a moral judgment with astonishing speed.

A pregnant person experiences anxiety.

Their child later struggles with attention.

The temptation is to draw a line between the two and ask what the parent did wrong.

But the evidence does not justify this kind of retrospective certainty.

People are not laboratory instruments whose emotional states can be maintained within a scientifically optimal range.

They live through divorces, bereavement, financial pressure, unsafe housing, discrimination, work demands, illness, relationship conflict, war, migration, caregiving burdens, and ordinary daily frustration.

Some of those conditions can be changed by individual action.

Many cannot.

And even when distress is modifiable, the evidence does not justify telling people that every anxious thought places their child at risk.

Treating distress is worthwhile because distress itself matters.

The parent's wellbeing is not merely a means to improve the child's developmental score.

It is an end in its own right.

This is one of the philosophical commitments of this book:

> **We should not make parents responsible for preventing every statistically possible developmental risk.**

Science should help people act where action is justified.

It should also help them stop worrying where evidence does not justify worry.

---

# Becoming a parent changes the parent

The transformation of parenthood is not confined to the developing child.

The adults change too.

Pregnancy is accompanied by profound endocrine changes, and longitudinal neuroimaging studies have documented changes in maternal brain structure across the transition to motherhood. Research has identified reductions in cortical gray matter volume in networks involved in social cognition and mentalizing, with changes persisting well beyond pregnancy.

These findings are easy to sensationalize.

They are often described in popular language as if pregnancy causes a loss of brain tissue or a decline in intelligence.

That is not what the evidence establishes.

Structural change is not synonymous with damage.

The adult brain is plastic.

It remodels in response to major biological and behavioral demands.

Motherhood represents one such demand.

And the story does not belong exclusively to mothers.

Longitudinal studies of first-time fathers have also identified changes in cortical structure, particularly in networks involved in social cognition, mentalizing, and caregiving. The magnitude and pattern of these changes appear related in part to caregiving engagement and other aspects of the transition to fatherhood.

That observation carries a larger implication.

The parental brain is not simply inherited.

It is also developed.

Adults enter parenthood with histories, personalities, biological characteristics, and learned patterns. Then they enter an environment that asks their brains to perform new kinds of work.

To notice a baby's signals.

To predict what a cry means.

To regulate one's own fear.

To remain responsive while exhausted.

To think from another person's perspective.

To become responsible for someone whose needs are initially almost entirely dependent on others.

Parenthood is therefore not only the beginning of a child's developmental story.

It is also a developmental transition for the adults.

---

# The relationship changes too

There is another environment that changes before the child can participate in it directly: the relationship between the adults who will care for that child.

The transition to parenthood is associated, on average, with deterioration in several measures of relationship functioning, particularly after the birth of a first child. But the averages conceal enormous variation. Some couples experience substantial strain. Others maintain stability. Some report strengthened connection. And relationship satisfaction can decline over time even among couples who do not become parents.

That last point is methodologically important.

If relationships decline after a baby arrives, how much of that decline is caused by parenthood itself?

How much would have happened anyway?

How much results from sleep deprivation?

How much from financial pressure?

How much from unequal distribution of domestic labor?

How much from health complications?

How much from lack of family support?

There is no single answer.

Parenthood interacts with trajectories that were already in motion.

This is why the book will resist a simplistic idea of "the effect of becoming a parent."

There is a transition.

But there is not one transition.

Different families move through it differently.

---

# What parents inherit—and what they build

Perhaps the deepest misconception in developmental science is the old idea that nature and nurture are two competing forces.

They are not.

Genes do not operate outside environments.

Environments do not operate on genetically identical blank slates.

The child arrives with inherited biology.

The child's biology then participates in shaping how environments are experienced.

Parents provide genes.

They also provide—or help create—relationships, language, routines, nutrition, protection, opportunities, expectations, and social models.

The child, meanwhile, changes the parents.

A difficult temperament can alter family routines.

An unusually responsive infant can strengthen particular patterns of interaction.

Sleep deprivation can change parental wellbeing.

A child's developing abilities can change what parents expect and how they behave.

The direction of influence is therefore not one-way.

It is transactional.

This matters because simplistic explanations tend to assign causation in one direction: parent to child.

Real families are more complicated.

The child affects the parent.

The parent affects the child.

Both are affected by the surrounding environment.

And all of them change over time.

---

# Early does not mean permanent

Developmental science has another uncomfortable lesson.

An effect measured early in life is not necessarily an effect that lasts.

This is particularly clear in intervention research.

A global systematic review of 102 randomized trials of parenting interventions found positive effects across several early developmental domains, including cognition, language, motor development, socioemotional outcomes, attachment, parenting knowledge, and parent-child interaction. The pooled effects were generally small to moderate, and substantial heterogeneity remained across interventions, settings, and outcomes.

These are important findings.

They tell us that aspects of the caregiving environment can be changed.

They also tell us something more subtle.

Some effects fade.

Follow-up studies of early interventions have found that gains in one period may diminish later, while other effects persist or emerge in different domains. The existence of an early effect therefore cannot be treated as proof of permanence.

The reverse is also true.

The disappearance of one measured advantage does not mean an intervention "did nothing."

Development can move effects from one pathway to another.

An intervention might produce a temporary cognitive gain but a longer-lasting change in behavior.

It might improve parent-child interaction without producing a measurable difference on a standardized test.

It might alter educational attainment, health, or life circumstances years later.

The right question is therefore never simply:

**Did it work?**

It is:

**What changed, for whom, by how much, for how long, under what conditions, and compared with what?**

That is a more demanding question.

It is also the question this book will keep asking.

---

# When the system matters more than the advice

Consider the Nurse-Family Partnership.

Early American trials provided evidence of meaningful benefits from intensive home visiting for socially and economically disadvantaged families. But when an adapted version was rigorously evaluated in England—where families already had access to comparatively strong universal public-health services—the added intervention produced little or no improvement on its principal short-term outcomes.

At first glance, these findings appear contradictory.

They are not.

An intervention does not operate in empty space.

Its effect depends partly on what exists without it.

If the control group already receives high-quality health visiting, social support, and access to services, the additional benefit of a specialized programme may be small.

The same intervention can therefore produce a meaningful benefit in one environment and little additional benefit in another.

This is one of the most important ideas in the entire book:

> **An intervention is never simply an intervention. It is an intervention within a system.**

The same principle appears in childcare policy.

Large-scale evidence from Quebec's subsidized childcare policy illustrates how a policy can produce mixed short-term developmental outcomes while generating important longer-term educational and economic effects, particularly among disadvantaged children.

Again, the story cannot be reduced to "daycare is good" or "daycare is bad."

Quality matters.

Context matters.

Baseline family conditions matter.

Economic security matters.

What happens to the parent may matter.

What happens to the child may differ.

And effects can change across time.

A policy is therefore not judged solely by whether it makes one developmental score move in the desired direction.

We must ask what problem the policy is solving, for whom, at what cost, and over what period.

---

# The responsibility question

This brings us to the most difficult question.

If development is influenced by prenatal biology, parent wellbeing, caregiving, relationships, and social conditions, who is responsible for creating a good developmental environment?

The instinctive answer is the parents.

Parents certainly matter.

But that answer is too small.

Suppose a pregnant person needs mental-health care but cannot afford it.

Suppose a parent knows that sleep would help but returns to work because leave is unavailable.

Suppose a family wants high-quality childcare but cannot afford it.

Suppose a child lives in unsafe housing.

Suppose the family has food insecurity, unreliable transport, or no access to culturally appropriate health services.

These are not failures of parenting technique.

They are conditions surrounding parenting.

The WHO's nurturing-care framework explicitly locates healthy development within a network of health, nutrition, security, responsive caregiving, and learning opportunities.

That means the developmental environment has multiple authors.

Parents write part of it.

Partners write part of it.

Extended family writes part of it.

Clinicians write part of it.

Childcare workers write part of it.

Employers, schools, communities, and governments write part of it too.

A serious account of child development must therefore resist the convenient habit of assigning institutional problems to individual parents.

The family is an important developmental system.

It is not the entire system.

---

# Science, interpretation, art

At this point, three different activities begin to separate.

**Science** asks what can be observed and tested.

**Interpretation** asks what those findings mean for a particular person or family.

**Art** concerns what cannot be settled by a study design: how people understand one another, what they value, how they make decisions under uncertainty, what kind of family they wish to build, and how they live with the fact that another human being can never be completely optimized or predicted.

These domains overlap.

But they are not interchangeable.

Science can tell us that folic acid reduces neural tube defects.

Interpretation tells us that this justifies making effective supplementation accessible without implying control over all future development.

Art begins where the evidence stops telling us exactly what kind of parent to become.

Science can tell us that parenthood changes adults.

It cannot tell us exactly how two particular people should divide their nights, negotiate conflict, preserve intimacy, balance work, or grieve the loss of their previous identity.

Science can identify the conditions under which a responsive-caregiving intervention works.

It cannot turn responsiveness into a perfect script.

That is why this book needs both halves of its title.

**Science** tells us what can reasonably be concluded.

**Art** concerns what we do with that knowledge.

---

# A different kind of parenting book

Much parenting advice begins with certainty.

Do this.

Never do that.

Begin now.

Do not miss the window.

Optimize the environment.

Maximize stimulation.

Prevent the problem before it occurs.

The developmental literature tells a different story.

Some things matter enormously.

Some matter modestly.

Some apparent effects are confounded.

Some interventions work.

Some do not.

Some work in one setting and not another.

Some early effects disappear.

Others persist.

Some risks can be reduced.

Others cannot be controlled at the family level at all.

And almost nothing is guaranteed.

That is not a disappointing conclusion.

It is an invitation to think differently.

The objective is not to create perfect parents capable of controlling every variable.

It is to understand which variables deserve attention.

Which deserve support.

Which deserve further research.

And which should be left out of the parent's burden.

---

# The child who has not yet arrived

Imagine a couple preparing for their first child.

They may already be making choices.

They may be considering health, work, money, where they will live, who will help them, whether they are ready, whether their relationship is strong enough, whether they know enough.

They may also be worrying.

About age.

About diet.

About stress.

About toxins.

About medications.

About sleep.

About whether they are already getting something wrong.

Some of those concerns deserve action.

Others deserve perspective.

A good developmental framework must be capable of doing both.

It must tell parents when an intervention is genuinely useful.

And it must be willing to say:

**This finding does not justify worrying about yourself.**

The science supports preparation.

It does not support obsession.

It supports risk reduction.

It does not support the fantasy of total control.

It supports caring for the pregnant person's health.

It does not support making every emotion a threat to the fetus.

It supports involving fathers and other caregivers.

It does not support creating another system of parental blame.

It supports early intervention.

It does not support assuming that every difference visible in infancy will determine adulthood.

And it supports something even more important:

**The story remains open.**

---

# Beginning before the beginning

The child who will eventually speak, walk, laugh, argue, remember, love, and become recognizably themselves does not arrive all at once.

There is no single developmental starting gun.

There are overlapping beginnings.

Gametes carry biological histories.

Conception creates a new developmental trajectory.

Pregnancy establishes a complex physiological environment.

Birth changes the sensory and social world.

Caregiving reorganizes that world again.

Language transforms it.

Relationships expand it.

Institutions structure it.

And the child participates in the entire process.

This is why the first chapter of a book about the first three years of life begins before birth.

But we must begin carefully.

The prenatal period is important without being all-powerful.

Parents matter without being omnipotent.

Genes matter without becoming destiny.

The environment matters without becoming a single explanation.

Early experience matters without becoming permanent by definition.

And interventions matter without becoming commandments.

The most scientifically defensible picture is also, perhaps, the most humane.

Human development begins before birth.

It continues after birth.

It remains plastic.

It remains relational.

It remains partly unpredictable.

And it remains open to change.

The child has not yet arrived.

The story has already begun.

But it has not yet been written.
